// This makes `vision/` a project, so `@/packages/bytes/src/buf.wac` in a file under here resolves
// to `vision/packages/bytes/src/buf.wac` rather than being a compile error.
//
// `@/` is the root of the project containing the *importing file* — the nearest directory at or
// above it holding a `wac.json5` — so a file in `vision/` never reaches out of `vision/`, and the
// code reads as though this directory were the repository. The corresponding real thing is always
// at the same path with `vision/` taken off the front.
//
// Empty is a valid manifest and this one is empty on purpose: its presence is the whole question
// `@/` asks, and nothing reads its contents to answer. The repository root has no manifest, which
// is why nothing outside `vision/` writes `@/` today.
//
// **And some imports across the directory disagree with the paragraph above.** Measured 2026-09-04
// as seventeen references to twelve distinct files; **re-measured 2026-09-05 and it is eight
// references to eight distinct files**, one apiece:
//
//     ./atof.wac      (fmt)        ./huffman.wac  ./crc32.wac   (gzip)
//     ./case.wac  ./printable.wac  (unicode)      ./percent.wac  ./host.wac  (url)
//     ./directory.wac (tor)
//
// Every one exists in `packages/` at the same path with `vision/` taken off the front.
//
// **The count fell because the directory kept writing, not because anything was fixed.**
// `@/packages/box/src/lib/args.wac` was four of the seventeen and exists here now; `./response.wac`,
// `./headers.wac`, `./routes.wac` and `./stringify.wac` were six more and arrived with the `http`,
// `server` and `json` rewrites. `./directory.wac` is new since, from `tor`. So the residue is the
// files nobody has had a reason to write, and the overlay question below is unchanged by the
// arithmetic — it is about what the resolver would have to do, not about how many sites want it.
//
// **Third value for one number, and the paragraph at the foot of this file predicted it.** It says
// *"a count in a comment ages against work that has nothing to do with it — which is the argument
// for the numbers living beside the method that produces them"*, and it was written when the number
// moved from 16/11 to 17/12. It has now moved to 8/8, for the same reason, in the same direction,
// under the note warning about it. The method is one script over the import graph and it takes a
// second; **what is missing is not the number, it is that nothing runs the script and rewrites this
// comment.** Neither will anything, because this directory is prose by design — so the honest form
// is the one above: a date beside every figure, and no claim that the figure is current.
//
// So the code assumes an **overlay**: resolve here, fall through to the repository if absent. That
// is not what this manifest says and it is not something the resolver can express.
//
// **Eleven of the twelve are `./` siblings and one is an `@/` import**, which the earlier version of
// this note missed by counting only the `@/` form. That matters: `@/` at least *names* a project
// root a resolver could be told to chain, and `./stringify.wac` names the directory the importing
// file is in. An overlay for relative imports is a different and larger thing — it means a file's
// own directory is two directories — and no manifest key could express it, which is why the entry in
// `../QUESTIONS.md` is a language question rather than a tidy-up.
//
// (An earlier count said sixteen names in eleven files and gave `@/packages/crypto/src/sha256.wac`
// as an example. That file was written on 2026-09-04 and five references with it, so the example is
// gone and the count moved for a reason unrelated to the question. A count in a comment ages against
// work that has nothing to do with it — which is the argument for the numbers living beside the
// method that produces them.)
//
// See `vision/packages/README.md`. Nothing in here compiles.
{}
